<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Social Security Act 1938</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Social Security Act 1938 was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> law passed by the first Labour government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-joseph-savage">Michael Joseph Savage</a>. It created a comprehensive system of state support, including old-age, invalid and widows' benefits, family allowances and free hospital treatment, funded by a special tax. It is regarded as the foundation of the New Zealand welfare state.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> already had some benefits from the Liberal Government, a tax-funded, means-tested old-age pension of 1898 and a widows' benefit of 1911, and the United/Reform Coalition had made limited payments to the unemployed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>. After winning the 1935 election the First Labour Government paid the unemployed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> bonus, but a regular unemployment benefit came only with this act.</p><p>The shape of the scheme divided the government. The finance minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-nash">Walter Nash</a>, proposed a contributory national insurance scheme, which the caucus rejected. Arnold Nordmeyer chaired the caucus committee and the parliamentary select committee that recommended a means-tested pension, universal superannuation and universal medical benefits, all paid for from direct taxation; at his insistence health and pensions were joined in one measure.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Savage outlined the bill in a broadcast on 2 April 1938, promising security unsurpassed in any other country and stressing that it had been carefully costed. It set a means-tested old-age pension of £78 a year for women over 60 and men over 65, a national superannuation of £10 a year for everyone aged 65 and over, rising each year towards the pension, continued or higher allowances for the unemployed, widows, orphans, veterans and the disabled, and a health system with free hospital treatment and medicine, a maternity benefit and subsidised doctors' visits. Income tax on wages rose from 8d to 1s in the pound, and a new Social Security Department took over the Pensions Department.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Savage adopted the phrase applied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, suggested by the Reverend W. H. A. Vickery, mayor of Kaiapoi. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> leader Adam Hamilton objected that nothing would be free, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidney-holland">Sidney Holland</a>'s jibe of applied lunacy drew public displeasure. The British Medical Association's New Zealand branch refused state fees for consultations until a compromise in 1941. A 1939 report for the Roosevelt administration called the scheme the first national attempt to combine protection against all such hazards in one system.</p><p>The act was not to come into force until 1 April 1939, leaving the opposition the chance to repeal it if it won the election. Labour made it the centre of the 1938 campaign and raised its vote from 45 to 55 percent, though it gained no seats. The scheme cost an estimated £17.85 million in its first year and shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> society until the early 1990s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Social Security Act 1938” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A comprehensive welfare state, founded by the <b>Social Security Act 1938</b>, provided pensions, family benefits and free hospital care, and full employment lasted into the 1970s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The first Labour government under Michael Joseph Savage, from 1935, built state housing and the <b>Social Security Act</b> of 1938.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-new-zealand">Dominion of New Zealand</a></li></ul></section>
